{{!
    @template theme_dimensions/category_listing

    One page of the category showcase: the strip of the category's pages, the
    toolbar partial (whose row under the filter line prints the live count of
    this page result),
    then a two-column shell, the navigation tree on the left and the content on
    the right. The content is the grid of card partials, or the empty state,
    followed by core's paging bar as real links. The view is one class on the
    container: cards or list. The category's own description is the hero's
    (category_hero), above this listing.

    The pages strip (category_pages) is drawn only when local_page is installed
    and names a page. The page builds it and the web service never does: nothing
    a search rewrites holds it.

    The tree is a plain list of links, this category first, then every admitted
    category below it down to the depth the site configured. It is a filter and
    not a way out: each entry is an address of this same page carrying the
    tree's own parameter, so following one narrows the listing to that
    category's subtree, and the first entry - this category's address without
    that parameter - clears it. The entry in force carries aria-current. Depth
    travels as a number on the item, which the stylesheet indents by; an entry
    the viewer may not open - a child merely listed on the anonymous shell - is
    text, so the name reads as it did and cannot be chosen.

    The whole tree is a native disclosure, open by default: from the
    stylesheet's breakpoint up its summary is hidden and the list simply shows,
    and on a phone the client closes it at load so that a long list of
    categories does not push the courses below the fold. With no JavaScript on a
    phone it stays open: every name is readable and the reader scrolls.

    Only the flat organisations draw the tree. Under "by area" the posters name
    the same children and open their courses, so the exporter sends no entries
    and the shell is one column.

    The grouped organisation draws two sections. The first is the areas: one
    poster per direct child and, between their rows, the panel of the open one
    (category_areas). The second is the courses sitting directly in this
    category, under their own heading, holding the grid, empty state and pager;
    the skip link beside the areas' heading lands on that heading. That section
    is hidden - never removed, because the client repaints its grid - when
    nothing sits directly here, under a criterion too: a narrowed page shows
    where its results are, and the areas say so themselves when there are none
    anywhere. With no child at all there are no posters and the page is the
    second section alone.

    The container carries the vitrine attribute, a JSON payload of the page's
    state that the client module reads once; the page renders correctly without
    it.

    The grid and the empty state are both always in the document, one of them
    hidden, because the client replaces a page of results with a page that may
    have none and back again. The pager region holds three things in turn:
    core's numbered bar, which the client hides; a "load more" button, drawn
    only for the client and hidden until there is a next page; and a link to the
    next numbered page, hidden until the live-page window closes and the client
    hands paging back to the real addresses. Without JavaScript only the bar is
    ever visible. The two client controls carry dim-live-only, which either
    client's mark on the body reveals: the logged-in one and the visitor's.

    The example draws both shells at once - a tree and the areas - so that every
    region is rendered and linted from one context. A real request carries one or
    the other.

